# CI/CD pipeline

`.gitlab-ci.yml` defines almost no jobs of its own. The lint, validate, test,
security and review jobs are `include`d from **{{ lib_project }}** at
`{{ lib_ref }}`, so a tooling change is made once in the library and picked up
here by moving that ref. Each template's inputs are documented in the library's
`docs/INCLUDE-CONTRACT.md`.

Only the **deploy** jobs are local: they are the part that touches this
cluster's hosts.

`include: project:` resolves on the GitLab instance the pipeline runs on, so
the library has to exist there as `{{ lib_project }}`. If this cluster does not
live on the instance that hosts the library, mirror or fork it and set
`lib_project` accordingly. `ansible/requirements.yml` and the Terraform module
sources are unaffected — they fetch over https from `{{ lib_url_ }}`.

## Stages

| Stage | Jobs | Source |
|---|---|---|
| `lint` | `yaml-lint`, `ansible-lint`, `shellcheck`, `python-lint`, `terraform-fmt`, `flux-lint` | library |
| `validate` | `terraform-validate` | library |
| `test` | `python-tests` (the cluster invariants in `tests/`) | library |
| `security` | `secret_detection` | library |
| `ai-review` | `pr-agent-review` | library |
| `deploy` | `deploy-ansible-base`, `deploy-ansible-k3s`, `deploy-terraform` | this repo |
| `maintenance` | `version-bump-bot` | library, scheduled |

Nothing in `kubernetes/` is deployed by CI. Flux reconciles it from `main`;
`flux-lint` is the gate that keeps an unreconcilable commit off that branch.

### flux-lint

Runs in **substitute mode**: it mirrors what kustomize-controller does at
reconcile time. Both postBuild ConfigMaps —
`kubernetes/infrastructure/sources/versions-configmap.yaml` and
`kubernetes/infrastructure/sources/cluster-config.yaml` — are exported by
`scripts/flux-render.sh`, every `${placeholder}` in a built Kustomization must
resolve to one of their keys, and the substituted output is schema-validated
with kubeconform.

A placeholder that resolves to nothing is the failure this catches: Flux and
`envsubst` both render an unknown variable as an **empty string**, so without
the gate a typo reaches the cluster as a manifest with a blank hostname.

`scripts/flux-render.sh` here is a superset of the library's copy — it accepts
several ConfigMaps. Do not replace it with the library version.

## Prerequisites

### Runner

Every job is tagged `{{ runner_tag }}`. `flux-lint` (substitute mode) and the
deploy jobs need a **root-capable** runner: they `apt-get install` and read
secrets with the 1Password CLI. If you split runners, keep those three on the
privileged one; the rest are non-root safe.

`secret_detection` pins `KUBERNETES_NODE_SELECTOR_CPU` to
`{{ ci_cpu_selector | default(internal_domain ~ '/cpu=modern') }}` — gitleaks
needs POPCNT/SSE4.2 and crashes on older CPUs. The value must satisfy the
runner's `node_selector_overwrite_allowed` regex, or the job fails at pod
creation; a runner that sets no regex ignores it.

### CI/CD variables

| Variable | Used by | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN` | every deploy job | Masked. Also the rule gate: with no token the deploy jobs are not created, so a fork's pipeline is green rather than broken. |
| `OPENAI__KEY` | `pr-agent-review` | Masked. Gates job creation together with the PAT. |
| `GITLAB__PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` | `pr-agent-review` | Masked, `api` scope — the account the review comments post as. |
| `VERSION_BUMP_BOT_TOKEN` | `version-bump-bot` | Masked PAT with `api` + `write_repository`. A job token cannot push, and the Merge requests API is read-only for it. |
| `GITHUB_TOKEN` | `version-bump-bot` | Optional, masked. Raises the GitHub API rate limit the version checker runs into once more than a handful of pins are tracked. |

### {{ onepassword_vault }} vault items

The deploy jobs read these at job time with `op`:

| Item / field | Used by |
|---|---|
| `SSH Key` → `private key`, `public key` | every Ansible deploy (the key Ansible connects with) |
| `Email Config` → `root_alias` | `deploy-ansible-base`, `deploy-ansible-k3s` |
| `SMTP Relay Auth` → `username`, `password` | `deploy-ansible-k3s` |
| `K3s Cluster Token` / `K3s Agent Token` → `credential` | `deploy-ansible-k3s` |
| `Loki Push Auth` → `username`, `password` | `deploy-ansible-k3s` |
| `Cloudflare Terraform Token` → `credential`, `username` | `deploy-terraform` |

Each deploy job's `op://` variables mirror the `env:` of the matching Taskfile
task, and that correspondence is load-bearing: `op run` resolves **every**
`op://` reference in the environment, so a reference to an item the vault does
not hold fails the job even when the playbook never reads it. Declare what the
playbook needs and nothing more.

Everything else a playbook needs is an `op://` reference in
`ansible/inventories/prod/group_vars/all.yml`, resolved by `op run --`.

## Deploys

Deploy jobs run on `main` only, and only when their own paths changed, so a
merge deploys exactly what it touched. `deploy-ansible-base` runs
automatically; `deploy-ansible-k3s` and `deploy-terraform` are **manual** —
both can disrupt the control plane or public DNS, so an operator triggers them
after reading the diff.

They are `interruptible: false` (a new pipeline must never cancel a running
deploy) and retry twice on `runner_system_failure` only — an evicted runner pod
is environmental and the playbooks are idempotent.

### Adding one

Add a job per playbook, following `deploy-ansible-base`:

```yaml
deploy-ansible-storage:
  stage: deploy
  extends: .deploy-base
  resource_group: deploy-ansible-storage      # serialize runs of THIS job
  script:
    - cd ansible
    - op run -- ansible-playbook -i inventories/prod playbooks/storage.yml
  environment:
    name: production/storage
  rules:
    - if: '$CI_PIPELINE_SOURCE == "schedule"'
      when: never
    - if: '$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == "main" && $OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN'
      changes:
        - "ansible/inventories/**/*"
        - "ansible/playbooks/storage.yml"
```

`.deploy-base` supplies the 1Password CLI, the pinned Ansible, a secure
`ansible.cfg` copy, the SSH key, an `ssh-keyscan` of every inventory host (from
`scripts/hosts.env`) and the galaxy install of `weisssrv.infra`.

The `changes:` list is the contract: a role that a playbook runs but whose path
is not listed here goes green on every change while never actually deploying.
When a playbook gains a role, extend the list in the same commit.

## Version bumps

`version-bump-bot` runs **only from a pipeline schedule** (or a manual web
run). It is the non-interactive twin of `task maintenance:update-all-versions`:
it clones the pinned library, runs its `check-versions.py --update-all` against
`scripts/version-registry.py`, regenerates the versions ConfigMap, and keeps
exactly one MR on `bot/version-bumps` in sync — new bumps force-push and create
or refresh the MR, unchanged content does nothing, and no bumps closes an open
MR. It never merges.

The bot's command list and the Taskfile's must stay in step; CI has no go-task,
which is why the commands are spelled out in `.gitlab-ci.yml` rather than
invoking the task. A pin the registry does not list is never checked — a fresh
cluster's registry is empty, so the bot correctly does nothing until you add
entries.

Create the schedule in **Settings → CI/CD → Schedules**: weekly is the intended
cadence, target `main`, no variables needed. Nothing else in the pipeline runs
on a schedule — every other job carries a `schedule → never` rule.

The report the checker writes (`version-report.json`) stays untracked and is
embedded in the MR description rather than committed.

## Bumping the library

Change every `ref:` in `.gitlab-ci.yml`, the `version:` in
`ansible/requirements.yml` and the `?ref=` on each Terraform module source to
the new tag, in one MR, and record it in `.copier-answers.yml` by re-running
`copier update`. There is no dependency bot for it — a library bump can change
job behaviour and role interfaces, so it is deliberate.

## Backend seams

The pipeline assumes two of the choices made when this repository was
generated:

- **`secrets_backend: {{ secrets_backend }}`** — `.deploy-base` installs the
  1Password CLI and reads the SSH key with `op read`, playbooks run under
  `op run --`, and `$OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN` gates every deploy rule. Another
  backend has to supply the same three things: a credential-fetch step in
  `.deploy-base`, a way to resolve the `op://`-shaped references in the
  inventory, and a variable to gate the deploy rules on.
- **`git_backend: {{ git_backend }}`** — the file is GitLab CI. A GitHub
  Actions equivalent would need workflows reproducing these jobs; the library
  ships GitLab CI templates only, so the shared half would have to be rebuilt
  as composite actions.
